# VS Code Fork Conventions

FewStepsAway is a fork of VS Code with a native AI integration layer, not a from-scratch app. Full context lives in `AGENTS.md` — this rule encodes its enforceable conventions.

## Isolation boundary

- **Minimize changes to core VS Code files.** Prefer extension points (services, contribution registries, commands, menus) over editing `src/vs/base`, `src/vs/editor`, `src/vs/workbench`, or `src/vs/platform` directly.
- **Keep AI code isolated under `src/vs/ai/`.** New AI functionality (providers, context management, indexing, completion, chat, refactoring, debug assist, search, extension API) belongs in the relevant `src/vs/ai/*` subfolder, not scattered into core modules.
- **Document any VS Code core modification** you do make — a short comment or a note in the PR description explaining why an extension point wasn't sufficient.

## TypeScript conventions

- **Strict types, no `any`.** Type function signatures fully (params and return type). If a type is genuinely unknown, use `unknown` and narrow it — never `any`.
- **Interfaces are prefixed `I`** (e.g. `IAIProvider`, `IContextManager`); **classes are not** (e.g. `OpenAIProvider implements IAIProvider`).
- **Dependency injection via VS Code's DI system.** Constructor-inject services with the `@IServiceName` decorator pattern, not manual instantiation or singletons reached for directly:

```typescript
export class ChatService implements IChatService {
    constructor(
        @IAIService private readonly aiService: IAIService,
        @IContextManager private readonly contextManager: IContextManager,
        @ILogService private readonly logService: ILogService
    ) { }
}
```

- **`Disposable` + `_register()` for cleanup.** Any class holding listeners, emitters, or other disposables extends `Disposable` and registers them via `this._register(...)` so they're torn down automatically:

```typescript
export class ContextManager extends Disposable implements IContextManager {
    private readonly _onContextChanged = this._register(new Emitter<ContextChange>());

    registerListeners(): void {
        this._register(this.editorService.onDidChange(() => this.handleChange()));
    }
}
```

- **`Emitter`/`Event` pattern for events**, not raw Node `EventEmitter` or ad-hoc callback props:

```typescript
export class MyService {
    private readonly _onEvent = new Emitter<MyEventType>();
    readonly onEvent: Event<MyEventType> = this._onEvent.event;

    triggerEvent(): void {
        this._onEvent.fire({ /* data */ });
    }
}
```

## File organization

- **One class/interface per file**, generally.
- **Group related files in folders**, exported through a barrel `index.ts`:

```typescript
// src/vs/ai/provider/common/index.ts
export * from './aiProvider';
export * from './providerRegistry';
export * from './rateLimiter';
```

## Naming conventions

| Type | Convention | Example |
|------|------------|---------|
| Interfaces | `IPascalCase` | `IAIProvider` |
| Classes | `PascalCase` | `OpenAIProvider` |
| Methods/Variables | `camelCase` | `sendRequest` |
| Constants | `UPPER_SNAKE_CASE` | `MAX_CONTEXT_TOKENS` |
| Enums | `PascalCase` | `AIMode` |
| Type aliases | `PascalCase` | `AIRequestContext` |

## Docs-first policy (mandatory)

Before modifying a core VS Code subsystem, or wiring a new contribution point (commands, keybindings, settings, menus), check the official VS Code extension/contribution API docs or an existing analogous pattern already in `src/vs/` — do not guess at undocumented internals of the upstream codebase. VS Code's internals change across upstream merges; a pattern that worked in an older VS Code version may no longer be the recommended one. If official docs are unavailable or ambiguous, say so explicitly rather than guessing silently.
